/-
zed-kask research pin (research run 4d38e9250a4edaa4): structural lemmas for
mathematical paths from an embedding vector to a LoRA adapter or a direct
weight edit (Qwen3-Embedding → 35B-A3B-class target).

Std-only, like every pin in this project: no Mathlib (lake-manifest
packages = []), so the commutative ring, the big sums, and the matrix
entries are all modeled here over bare functions. Core Lean 4 only;
checked with the project's pinned Lake toolchain via lean_check. (Note:
`ℕ` notation is Mathlib — this file spells out `Nat`.)

STATED ASSUMPTIONS (project ground rules, prompt §2 rule 3):
1. `RingF` models a commutative ring — the standard commutative-ring facts,
   each a class field; R and Q are intended models. Idealized
   finite-dimensional linear algebra over a ring: no quantization, no
   runtime, and NO behavioral claims about any model.
2. "rank(B·A) ≤ r" is pinned in its generating form (`lora_entry`): every
   entry of B·A is Σ_k A_{kj}·B_{ik}, i.e. each column of B·A is a linear
   combination of the r columns of B. The dimension-theoretic reading
   (column-space dimension ≤ r) is standard linear algebra, reported as
   interpretation, not formalized here.
3. The bridge of `bridge_linear` is the contraction
   v ↦ (i j ↦ Σ_k T i j k · v k); linearity in v is the pinned form of "the
   image family is generated by the d basis images" (standard linear
   algebra; interpretation), which underlies the ≤ d-dimensional-family
   claim of the H2 branch.
4. Vectors and matrices are bare functions Nat → R / Nat → Nat → R;
   dimensions enter as explicit summation bounds. Any function may be
   supplied; the bounds do the cutting.
-/

namespace EmbedToAdapter

/-- A commutative ring: the stated assumptions, as standard facts. -/
class RingF (R : Type) where
  add : R → R → R
  mul : R → R → R
  zero : R
  add_comm : ∀ (a b : R), add a b = add b a
  add_assoc : ∀ (a b c : R), add (add a b) c = add a (add b c)
  add_zero : ∀ (a : R), add a zero = a
  zero_add : ∀ (a : R), add zero a = a
  mul_comm : ∀ (a b : R), mul a b = mul b a
  mul_assoc : ∀ (a b c : R), mul (mul a b) c = mul a (mul b c)
  left_distrib : ∀ (a b c : R), mul a (add b c) = add (mul a b) (mul a c)
  mul_zero : ∀ (a : R), mul a zero = zero
  zero_mul : ∀ (a : R), mul zero a = zero

open RingF

variable {R : Type} [RingF R]

-- ── Derived closure facts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

theorem add_left_comm (a b c : R) : add a (add b c) = add b (add a c) := by
  rw [← add_assoc, ← add_assoc, add_comm b a]

theorem mul_add (a b c : R) : mul (add a b) c = add (mul a c) (mul b c) := by
  rw [mul_comm (add a b) c, left_distrib, mul_comm c a, mul_comm c b]

-- ── Bounded sums: Σ_{k<n} f k, by recursion on the bound ─────────────────────

/-- `sumTo f n` = Σ_{k<n} f k. -/
def sumTo (f : Nat → R) : Nat → R
  | 0 => zero
  | n + 1 => add (sumTo f n) (f n)

theorem sumTo_congr (f g : Nat → R) (n : Nat) (h : ∀ k, k < n → f k = g k) :
    sumTo f n = sumTo g n := by
  induction n with
  | zero => rfl
  | succ n ih =>
      have h1 : f n = g n := h n (by omega)
      have h2 : ∀ k, k < n → f k = g k := fun k hk => h k (by omega)
      show add (sumTo f n) (f n) = add (sumTo g n) (g n)
      rw [ih h2, h1]

theorem sumTo_zero (n : Nat) : sumTo (fun _ => (zero : R)) n = (zero : R) := by
  induction n with
  | zero => rfl
  | succ n ih =>
      show add (sumTo (fun _ => zero) n) zero = zero
      rw [ih, add_zero]

theorem sumTo_add (f g : Nat → R) (n : Nat) :
    sumTo (fun k => add (f k) (g k)) n = add (sumTo f n) (sumTo g n) := by
  induction n with
  | zero => exact (add_zero zero).symm
  | succ n ih =>
      show add (sumTo (fun k => add (f k) (g k)) n) (add (f n) (g n))
         = add (add (sumTo f n) (f n)) (add (sumTo g n) (g n))
      rw [ih]
      simp [add_comm, add_left_comm]

theorem sumTo_mul_left (c : R) (f : Nat → R) (n : Nat) :
    sumTo (fun k => mul c (f k)) n = mul c (sumTo f n) := by
  induction n with
  | zero => exact (mul_zero c).symm
  | succ n ih =>
      show add (sumTo (fun k => mul c (f k)) n) (mul c (f n))
         = mul c (add (sumTo f n) (f n))
      rw [ih, ← left_distrib]

-- ── The edit-path primitives ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

/-- matvec over input dimension `n`: row `i` of `W` dotted with `x`. -/
def matVec (W : Nat → Nat → R) (n : Nat) (x : Nat → R) (i : Nat) : R :=
  sumTo (fun j => mul (W i j) (x j)) n

/-- Outer product `u ⊗ k`. -/
def outer (u k : Nat → R) (i j : Nat) : R := mul (u i) (k j)

/-- Dot product `k ⬝ x` over dimension `n`. -/
def dot (k x : Nat → R) (n : Nat) : R := sumTo (fun j => mul (k j) (x j)) n

/-- The linear bridge: contraction of a 3-tensor `T` with `v` over `d`
(the H2 seeded-factorization primitive). -/
def bridge (T : Nat → Nat → Nat → R) (d : Nat) (v : Nat → R) (i j : Nat) : R :=
  sumTo (fun k => mul (T i j k) (v k)) d

/-- Factored-update entry `(B · A)_{ij}` over rank bound `r` (LoRA form). -/
def mulEntry (B : Nat → Nat → R) (A : Nat → Nat → R) (r : Nat) (i j : Nat) : R :=
  sumTo (fun k => mul (B i k) (A k j)) r

-- ── T1 (H2 structure): the bridge is linear in the embedding vector ─────────

/-- Linearity of the seeded bridge in `v`: the family {bridge T d v} is a
linear family in `v`, hence (standard interpretation) generated by the `d`
basis images — the pinned form of "a linear bridge yields a ≤
d-dimensional family of adapters". -/
theorem bridge_linear (T : Nat → Nat → Nat → R) (d : Nat) (a b : R) (u w : Nat → R)
    (i j : Nat) :
    bridge T d (fun k => add (mul a (u k)) (mul b (w k))) i j
      = add (mul a (bridge T d u i j)) (mul b (bridge T d w i j)) := by
  have hAc : ∀ k, mul (T i j k) (mul a (u k)) = mul a (mul (T i j k) (u k)) := by
    intro k
    rw [← mul_assoc, mul_comm (T i j k) a, mul_assoc]
  have hBc : ∀ k, mul (T i j k) (mul b (w k)) = mul b (mul (T i j k) (w k)) := by
    intro k
    rw [← mul_assoc, mul_comm (T i j k) b, mul_assoc]
  have stepA : sumTo (fun k => mul (T i j k) (mul a (u k))) d
      = mul a (sumTo (fun k => mul (T i j k) (u k)) d) := by
    rw [sumTo_congr (fun k => mul (T i j k) (mul a (u k)))
                     (fun k => mul a (mul (T i j k) (u k))) d
                     (fun k _ => hAc k),
        sumTo_mul_left a (fun k => mul (T i j k) (u k)) d]
  have stepB : sumTo (fun k => mul (T i j k) (mul b (w k))) d
      = mul b (sumTo (fun k => mul (T i j k) (w k)) d) := by
    rw [sumTo_congr (fun k => mul (T i j k) (mul b (w k)))
                     (fun k => mul b (mul (T i j k) (w k))) d
                     (fun k _ => hBc k),
        sumTo_mul_left b (fun k => mul (T i j k) (w k)) d]
  show sumTo (fun k => mul (T i j k) (add (mul a (u k)) (mul b (w k)))) d
       = add (mul a (sumTo (fun k => mul (T i j k) (u k)) d))
             (mul b (sumTo (fun k => mul (T i j k) (w k)) d))
  simp only [left_distrib]
  rw [sumTo_add, stepA, stepB]

-- ── T2 (arm (a) lands in the target set): the LoRA rank bound ────────────────

/-- `(B · A)_{ij} = Σ_k A_{kj} · B_{ik}`: every column of the factored update
is a linear combination of the `r` columns of `B` — the pinned form of
"every LoRA update has rank ≤ r". -/
theorem lora_entry (B A : Nat → Nat → R) (r i j : Nat) :
    mulEntry B A r i j = sumTo (fun k => mul (A k j) (B i k)) r := by
  show sumTo (fun k => mul (B i k) (A k j)) r
       = sumTo (fun k => mul (A k j) (B i k)) r
  exact sumTo_congr _ _ r (fun k _ => mul_comm (B i k) (A k j))

-- ── T3 (H3 compilation): what a rank-one edit does ──────────────────────────

/-- A rank-one edit `W + u ⊗ k` acts on input `x` by adding the scalar
`(k ⬝ x)` times `u` — the input-scaled injection of `u`. -/
theorem rank_one_edit_action (W : Nat → Nat → R) (u k x : Nat → R) (n i : Nat) :
    matVec (fun i' j => add (W i' j) (outer u k i' j)) n x i
      = add (matVec W n x i) (mul (dot k x n) (u i)) := by
  show sumTo (fun j => mul (add (W i j) (mul (u i) (k j))) (x j)) n
       = add (sumTo (fun j => mul (W i j) (x j)) n)
             (mul (sumTo (fun j => mul (k j) (x j)) n) (u i))
  simp only [mul_add]
  rw [sumTo_add]
  simp only [mul_assoc]
  rw [sumTo_mul_left (u i) (fun j => mul (k j) (x j)) n]
  rw [mul_comm (sumTo (fun j => mul (k j) (x j)) n) (u i)]

/-- Corollary (locality, constraint C4): on inputs `x` orthogonal to the
trigger (`k ⬝ x = 0`), the rank-one edit changes the output by exactly
zero. -/
theorem rank_one_edit_preserves_orthogonal (W : Nat → Nat → R) (u k x : Nat → R)
    (n i : Nat) (h : dot k x n = zero) :
    matVec (fun i' j => add (W i' j) (outer u k i' j)) n x i
      = matVec W n x i := by
  have hact := rank_one_edit_action W u k x n i
  rw [h, zero_mul, add_zero] at hact
  exact hact

-- ── T4 (H3 obstruction): constant steering cannot be a weight edit ──────────

/-- No linear weight edit — LoRA or direct — can implement a nonzero
constant behavioral offset: instantiating at the zero input forces
`c = 0`. Constant steering compiles to a bias edit instead (arm (b)
only; not expressible as `B · A`). -/
theorem no_linear_edit_is_constant (W ΔW : Nat → Nat → R) (n i : Nat) (c : R)
    (hc : c ≠ zero) :
    ¬ ∀ x : Nat → R, matVec ΔW n x i = add (matVec W n x i) c := by
  intro h
  have h0 := h (fun _ => (zero : R))
  have ec1 : sumTo (fun j => mul (ΔW i j) (zero : R)) n = zero := by
    rw [sumTo_congr (fun j => mul (ΔW i j) (zero : R)) (fun _ => zero) n
                     (fun j _ => mul_zero (ΔW i j)),
        sumTo_zero]
  have ec2 : sumTo (fun j => mul (W i j) (zero : R)) n = zero := by
    rw [sumTo_congr (fun j => mul (W i j) (zero : R)) (fun _ => zero) n
                     (fun j _ => mul_zero (W i j)),
        sumTo_zero]
  have m1 : matVec ΔW n (fun _ => (zero : R)) i
      = sumTo (fun j => mul (ΔW i j) (zero : R)) n := rfl
  have m2 : matVec W n (fun _ => (zero : R)) i
      = sumTo (fun j => mul (W i j) (zero : R)) n := rfl
  rw [m1, m2, ec1, ec2, zero_add] at h0
  exact hc h0.symm

-- ── Merge semantics: a merged adapter IS a direct weight edit ────────────────

theorem sumTo_succ (f : Nat → R) (n : Nat) : sumTo f (n + 1) = add (sumTo f n) (f n) := rfl

theorem sumTo_mul_right (c : R) (f : Nat → R) (n : Nat) :
    sumTo (fun k => mul (f k) c) n = mul (sumTo f n) c := by
  induction n with
  | zero => exact (zero_mul c).symm
  | succ n ih =>
      show add (sumTo (fun k => mul (f k) c) n) (mul (f n) c)
         = mul (add (sumTo f n) (f n)) c
      rw [ih, ← mul_add]

theorem sumTo_exchange (f : Nat → Nat → R) (n r : Nat) :
    sumTo (fun j => sumTo (fun k => f j k) r) n
      = sumTo (fun k => sumTo (fun j => f j k) n) r := by
  induction n with
  | zero =>
      have e : ∀ k, sumTo (fun j => f j k) 0 = (zero : R) := fun _ => rfl
      rw [sumTo_congr (fun k => sumTo (fun j => f j k) 0) (fun _ => (zero : R)) r
                     (fun k _ => e k),
          sumTo_zero]
      rfl
  | succ n ih =>
      have hR : sumTo (fun k => sumTo (fun j => f j k) (n + 1)) r
          = add (sumTo (fun k => sumTo (fun j => f j k) n) r) (sumTo (fun k => f n k) r) := by
        rw [sumTo_congr (fun k => sumTo (fun j => f j k) (n + 1))
                         (fun k => add (sumTo (fun j => f j k) n) (f n k)) r
                         (fun k _ => sumTo_succ (fun j => f j k) n),
            sumTo_add]
      show add (sumTo (fun j => sumTo (fun k => f j k) r) n) (sumTo (fun k => f n k) r)
         = sumTo (fun k => sumTo (fun j => f j k) (n + 1)) r
      rw [ih, hR]

theorem mulEntry_eq (B A : Nat → Nat → R) (r i j : Nat) :
    mulEntry B A r i j = sumTo (fun k => mul (B i k) (A k j)) r := rfl

/-- T5 (merge semantics): applying the LoRA update by MERGING it into the
weights — W ← W + s · (B·A) with s = α/r — computes exactly W x + s · B(A x),
the LoRA runtime semantics. A merged adapter is a direct weight edit with
identical action in the idealized exact-arithmetic model. (Quantized merges
introduce rounding relative to runtime LoRA — an interpretation caveat
about real checkpoints, not a lemma.) Together with `lora_entry` (rank ≤ r)
this pins the adapter→direct-edit half of the synthesis question. -/
theorem lora_runtime_semantics (W B A : Nat → Nat → R) (r n : Nat) (s : R)
    (x : Nat → R) (i : Nat) :
    matVec (fun i' j => add (W i' j) (mul s (mulEntry B A r i' j))) n x i
      = add (matVec W n x i) (mul s (matVec B r (matVec A n x) i)) := by
  have step1 : ∀ j, mul (mul s (mulEntry B A r i j)) (x j)
      = mul s (sumTo (fun k => mul (B i k) (mul (A k j) (x j))) r) := by
    intro j
    have e : mul (sumTo (fun k => mul (B i k) (A k j)) r) (x j)
        = sumTo (fun k => mul (mul (B i k) (A k j)) (x j)) r :=
      (sumTo_mul_right (x j) (fun k => mul (B i k) (A k j)) r).symm
    rw [mulEntry_eq, mul_assoc, e]
    simp only [mul_assoc]
  have e2 : sumTo (fun j => mul (mul s (mulEntry B A r i j)) (x j)) n
      = mul s (matVec B r (matVec A n x) i) := by
    show sumTo (fun j => mul (mul s (mulEntry B A r i j)) (x j)) n
         = mul s (sumTo (fun k => mul (B i k) (sumTo (fun j => mul (A k j) (x j)) n)) r)
    rw [sumTo_congr (fun j => mul (mul s (mulEntry B A r i j)) (x j))
                     (fun j => mul s (sumTo (fun k => mul (B i k) (mul (A k j) (x j))) r)) n
                     (fun j _ => step1 j),
        sumTo_mul_left s (fun j => sumTo (fun k => mul (B i k) (mul (A k j) (x j))) r) n,
        sumTo_exchange (fun j k => mul (B i k) (mul (A k j) (x j))) n r,
        sumTo_congr (fun k => sumTo (fun j => mul (B i k) (mul (A k j) (x j))) n)
                     (fun k => mul (B i k) (sumTo (fun j => mul (A k j) (x j)) n)) r
                     (fun k _ => sumTo_mul_left (B i k) (fun j => mul (A k j) (x j)) n)]
  show sumTo (fun j => mul (add (W i j) (mul s (mulEntry B A r i j))) (x j)) n
       = add (sumTo (fun j => mul (W i j) (x j)) n)
             (mul s (matVec B r (matVec A n x) i))
  simp only [mul_add]
  rw [sumTo_add, e2]

end EmbedToAdapter

